# No Browser confirm() Dialogs

Never use `window.confirm()` or `confirm()` for destructive action confirmations. Always use a custom in-app confirmation dialog that matches the app's design.

## Pattern

Use a `ConfirmDialog` component (or inline state) with `.overlay` + `.confirm-dialog` CSS classes:

```jsx
// ✅ CORRECT — custom in-app dialog
const [confirmDialog, setConfirmDialog] = useState(null);

const handleDelete = (item) => {
  setConfirmDialog({
    message: `Excluir "${item.name}"? Esta ação não pode ser desfeita.`,
    onConfirm: async () => {
      setConfirmDialog(null);
      await api.delete(`/endpoint/${item.id}`);
    },
  });
};

// In JSX:
{confirmDialog && (
  <div className="overlay" onClick={() => setConfirmDialog(null)}>
    <div className="confirm-dialog" onClick={(e) => e.stopPropagation()}>
      <p className="confirm-dialog-message">{confirmDialog.message}</p>
      <div className="btn-group">
        <button onClick={confirmDialog.onConfirm} className="btn-danger" style={{ flex: 1 }}>
          Confirmar
        </button>
        <button onClick={() => setConfirmDialog(null)} className="btn-secondary" style={{ flex: 1 }}>
          Cancelar
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
)}

// ❌ WRONG
if (!confirm('Tem certeza?')) return;
```

CSS classes `.confirm-dialog` and `.confirm-dialog-message` are defined in `src/index.css`.
